From 7a65ca02c77dda0330619d6fc97da029aa15eeae Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kasper Olesen <kaspersolesen@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 00:13:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/1] mmc: dw_mmc-pltfm: socfpga: restore maximum data timeout

Install a socfpga-only `set_data_timeout` hook that always programs
TMOUT = 0xFFFFFFFF, restoring what MiSTer's 5.15 kernel did for every
data transfer.

WHAT CHANGED UNDER US
Mainline 6a8c2018e872 ("mmc: dw_mmc: Allow lower TMOUT value than
maximum", v5.17) stopped writing the maximum TMOUT unconditionally and
started deriving TMOUT[31:8] from `data->timeout_ns`:

    linux-6.18.52 drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc.c:1285-1314
        static void dw_mci_set_data_timeout(struct dw_mci *host,
                                            unsigned int timeout_ns)
        {
                const struct dw_mci_drv_data *drv_data = host->drv_data;
                ...
                if (drv_data && drv_data->set_data_timeout)
                        return drv_data->set_data_timeout(host, timeout_ns);
                ...
                if (!tmp || tmp > 0xFFFFFF)
                        tmout |= (0xFFFFFF << 8);
                else
                        tmout |= (tmp & 0xFFFFFF) << 8;
                mci_writel(host, TMOUT, tmout);

`mmc_set_data_timeout()` asks for 100 ms on an SD read, so on the
DE10-Nano's 50 MHz controller clock TMOUT reads back 0x4C4B40FF. The
software read timer is armed from that same register:

    linux-6.18.52 drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc.c:2003-2025
        static void dw_mci_set_drto(struct dw_mci *host)
        ...
                drto_clks = mci_readl(host, TMOUT) >> 8;
        ...
                drto_ms += 10;   /* add a bit spare time */

and with IDMAC there is no intermediate progress interrupt, so that
~110 ms budget has to cover an entire multi-block request -- up to 1 MB
at `read_ahead_kb=1024`. 5.15's maximum TMOUT gave ~346 ms.

WHY THE FAILURE IS SILENT
When a request misses the budget `mmc_blk_mq_rw_recovery()` retries it,
then resets the card and re-reads the request one sector at a time
(CMD17 + CMD13 per sector). dw_mmc does not update its debugfs
`err_stats` on this path and nothing prints -110; the only trace in
dmesg is a repeated

    Bus speed (slot 0) = 50000000Hz (slot req 400000Hz, actual 400000HZ ...)

from the card re-initialising. A user sees stutter, not an error.

Provenance
----------
Origin:       7a65ca02c77dda0330619d6fc97da029aa15eeae
              "mmc: dw_mmc-pltfm: socfpga: restore maximum data timeout
              (#105)", MiSTer-devel/Linux-Kernel_MiSTer, branch
              MiSTer-v6.18, PR #105, 2026-09-17. Refs the fork's own
              issue #103 (PSX CHD stutter / slow N64 loads), NOT
              confirmed as its cause -- see "What this does not prove"
              below.
Author:       Kasper Olesen <kaspersolesen@gmail.com> (GitHub @kblood).
              The fork commit's own Signed-off-by spells the surname
              "Kasper Solesen"; same person, same address -- reproduced
              below as he wrote it rather than silently corrected.
Upstream:     ABSENT from both kernels this repo builds, measured
              against the pinned, hash-verified tarballs rather than a
              mirror. `grep -n set_data_timeout drivers/mmc/host/
              dw_mmc-pltfm.c` returns nothing (exit 1) in linux-6.18.52
              and in linux-7.2.6; in both trees the only in-tree user of
              the hook is Exynos (dw_mmc-exynos.c:577 and :649), and
              socfpga_drv_data carries `.init` alone --
                  linux-6.18.52 drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc-pltfm.c:99-101
                  linux-7.2.6  drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc-pltfm.c:89-91
                      static const struct dw_mci_drv_data socfpga_drv_data = {
                              .init           = dw_mci_socfpga_priv_init,
                      };
              So this is not a backport: nothing upstream has ever
              carried it, and there is no mainline SHA to attribute.
              The hook itself IS upstream (dw_mmc.h:597,617 in 6.18.52;
              :517,:537 in 7.2.6), which is why no core change is
              needed.
Applies to:   BOTH series. The added lines are byte-identical on 6.18
              and 7.2; only the line numbers move, because 7.x rewrote
              dw_mci_socfpga_priv_init()'s clock-phase parsing ABOVE the
              anchor and left the anchor itself untouched. Measured: the
              hunk lands at offset 0 on 6.18.52 and at offset -10 on
              7.2.6, zero fuzz, so board/mister/de10nano/
              linux-patches-beta/ carries a SYMLINK to this file rather
              than a re-anchored copy.
DE25 exclusion: deliberately absent from board/mister/de25nano/
              linux-patches/. `socfpga_drv_data` is reached only through
              `{ .compatible = "altr,socfpga-dw-mshc", .data =
              &socfpga_drv_data, }` (dw_mmc-pltfm.c:105 / :95), and the
              Agilex 5 SD controller is a Cadence SDHCI part, not a
              DesignWare one -- see board/mister/de25nano/linux-patches/
              0101-mmc-sdhci-cadence-agilex5-40-bit-dma-mask.patch and
              board/mister/de25nano/linux.config:178-180
              (CONFIG_MMC_SDHCI / _PLTFM / _CADENCE=y). That config has
              no CONFIG_MMC_DW at all, so dw_mmc-pltfm.c is not even
              compiled for that board; the DE10 config has it at
              board/mister/de10nano/linux.config:510. Excluding it keeps
              the DE25 series honest rather than carrying inert code.
Forward-port: None needed -- applied as authored. The unused
              `timeout_ns` parameter matches the hook's prototype and
              the kernel does not build with -Wunused-parameter.
What this
does not
prove:        The PR reproduces the failure mode with an artificially
              shortened budget (30 ms), not with a card that fails at
              the stock 100 ms; the author estimates a card would have
              to be slower than ~9 MB/s on 1 MB requests, or show
              ~100 ms internal latency spikes, to trigger it as shipped.
              The cost of carrying it is bounded and known: a genuinely
              unresponsive card now takes ~346 ms instead of ~110 ms to
              fail a read. Boot-tested by the author on a DE10-Nano
              (TMOUT reads back 0xFFFFFFFF, exFAT ~22 MB/s and raw
              ~23 MB/s unchanged); NOT yet exercised on this project's
              rig.

Signed-off-by: Kasper Solesen <kaspersolesen@gmail.com>
Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Michael C. Ferguson <michael.christopher.ferguson@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc-pltfm.c | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc-pltfm.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc-pltfm.c
@@ -96,8 +96,18 @@ static int dw_mci_socfpga_priv_init(struct dw_mci *host)
 	return 0;
 }
 
+static void dw_mci_socfpga_set_data_timeout(struct dw_mci *host,
+					    unsigned int timeout_ns)
+{
+	/*
+	 * Pre-v5.17 behaviour: 100 ms is too short for a whole SD read request.
+	 */
+	mci_writel(host, TMOUT, 0xFFFFFFFF);
+}
+
 static const struct dw_mci_drv_data socfpga_drv_data = {
-	.init		= dw_mci_socfpga_priv_init,
+	.init			= dw_mci_socfpga_priv_init,
+	.set_data_timeout	= dw_mci_socfpga_set_data_timeout,
 };
 
 static const struct of_device_id dw_mci_pltfm_match[] = {
